  "solution": {
    "approach": "We simply need the maximum of three numbers for each test case. For each test case we read the three integers, keep a variable mx initialized to the first number and compare it with the second and third numbers, updating mx whenever a larger value is found. Finally we output \"Largest: mx\". This runs in O(1) time per test case and uses O(1) extra space.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(T) total, O(1) per test case",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) auxiliary space",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;import java.util.*;public class Main{public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception{BufferedReader br=new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));StringBuilder sb=new StringBuilder();int T=Integer.parseInt(br.readLine().trim());for(int t=0;t<T;t++){StringTokenizer st=new StringTokenizer(br.readLine());int a=Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());int b=Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());int c=Integer.parseInt(st.nextToken());int mx=a;if(b>mx)mx=b;if(c>mx)mx=c;sb.append(\"Largest: \").append(mx);if(t<T-1)sb.append('\\n');}System.out.print(sb.toString());}}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
